## Database Schema

All platform-related fields exist from day one but are nullable. Use generic naming — never Shopify-specific column names anywhere in the schema.

### merchants
```ruby
create_table :merchants, id: :uuid do |t|
  t.string   :name,              null: false
  t.string   :platform                          # 'shopify', 'woocommerce', 'amazon', null
  t.string   :platform_domain                   # store URL or seller ID, null until connected
  t.string   :access_token                      # encrypted, null until platform connected
  t.datetime :catalog_synced_at                 # null until platform connected
  t.timestamps
end
```

### suppliers
```ruby
create_table :suppliers, id: :uuid do |t|
  t.references :merchant, type: :uuid, null: false, foreign_key: true
  t.string     :name,     null: false
  t.timestamps
end
```

### variants
Local mirror of the merchant's master product catalog. In V1 populated via manual CSV import or manual UI entry. Platform fields are nullable and populated only when platform integration is built.

```ruby
create_table :variants, id: :uuid do |t|
  t.references :merchant,             type: :uuid, null: false, foreign_key: true
  t.string     :product_title,        null: false    # "Air Runner Sneaker"
  t.string     :variant_title,        null: false    # "Black / Size 9"
  t.string     :master_sku,           null: false    # "AERO-BLK-09"
  t.string     :barcode                             # EAN or UPC, nullable
  t.string     :platform                            # 'shopify', 'woocommerce', null
  t.string     :platform_variant_id                 # platform's own variant ID, null
  t.string     :platform_inventory_id               # platform's inventory item ID, null
  t.string     :status,               null: false, default: 'active'   # 'active', 'deleted'
  t.datetime   :synced_at                           # null until platform connected
  t.timestamps

  t.index [:merchant_id, :master_sku], unique: true
  t.index [:merchant_id, :barcode]
  t.index [:merchant_id, :platform_variant_id]
  t.index [:merchant_id, :status]
end
```

### supplier_profiles
Gemini interpreter cache. Once a profile exists for a supplier, Gemini never runs again unless drift is detected.

```ruby
create_table :supplier_profiles, id: :uuid do |t|
  t.references :merchant, type: :uuid, null: false, foreign_key: true
  t.references :supplier, type: :uuid, null: false, foreign_key: true
  t.string     :sku_column_name,      null: false    # "ItemCode_Ref"
  t.string     :quantity_column_name, null: false    # "Avail_Qty"
  t.jsonb      :raw_headers,          null: false    # full header array for drift detection
  t.datetime   :last_used_at
  t.timestamps

  t.index [:merchant_id, :supplier_id], unique: true
end
```

### mapping_dictionary
The core translation table. Every supplier code ever seen by the engine lives here once encountered.

```ruby
create_table :mapping_dictionaries, id: :uuid do |t|
  t.references :merchant, type: :uuid, null: false, foreign_key: true
  t.references :supplier, type: :uuid, null: false, foreign_key: true
  t.string     :supplier_code,        null: false    # "XYZ-9982"
  t.string     :master_sku                           # "AERO-BLK-09", display + auto-match reference
  t.string     :platform_variant_id                  # null until platform connected
  t.string     :status,               null: false, default: 'pending'   # 'active', 'skipped', 'pending'
  t.datetime   :last_seen                            # updated on every upload where this code appears
  t.timestamps

  t.index [:merchant_id, :supplier_id, :supplier_code], unique: true
  t.index [:merchant_id, :status]
  t.index :last_seen
end
```

---

## Status Field Reference

### mapping_dictionary.status
```
active   — mapped and confirmed by merchant, auto-resolve on every future upload
skipped  — merchant said they don't carry this, never surface again in any upload
pending  — seen but not yet mapped, reappear on next upload
```

### variants.status
```
active   — variant exists and is live in the merchant's catalog
deleted  — variant was removed, exclude from all output and sync
```

### output JSON status values
```
Ready_To_Sync    — fully resolved, has master_sku, ready for platform sync when that phase is built
Needs_Review     — supplier code could not be resolved, merchant attention required
variant_deleted  — was previously mapped but the target variant has since been deleted
```

---

## last_seen Behaviour

Every time a supplier code appears in an uploaded file, the mapping_dictionary row for that code has its last_seen updated to the current timestamp — regardless of whether the code resolved successfully or not.

Codes where last_seen is older than 180 days (6 months) surface in a merchant dashboard alert:
"These supplier codes haven't appeared in any recent upload — still relevant?"

Merchant can bulk-delete or bulk-skip stale mappings in one action. This prevents ghost mappings from accumulating over time.

